  verified_dates = {2026-07},
  audit_count  = {1},
  note         = {AI-generated contextual summary: Barlow's 1996 manifesto is the founding text of internet cyber-libertarianism and the load-bearing anchor of the great-figures Barlow dossier. Addressed to ``Governments of the Industrial World, you weary giants of flesh and steel'', it denies the nation-state any authority over the online realm: ``You have no sovereignty where we gather'', ``Cyberspace does not lie within your borders'', ``You have no moral right to rule us''. Governance, it argues, will instead ``arise according to the conditions of our world'' and emerge ``from ethics, enlightened self-interest, and the commonweal''; ``Your legal concepts of property, expression, identity, movement, and context do not apply to us. They are all based on matter, and there is no matter here.'' It closes on the utopian note ``We will create a civilization of the Mind in Cyberspace.'' The dossier draws on it across nearly every axis: Trust (institutions denied legitimacy, TRU floor), Centralisation (radical decentralism, CEN floor), Control (radical freedom, CON floor), Optimism (civilisational good), and Transhumanism (the Internet as the ``new home of Mind'', identities with ``no bodies'').},
}

