  note            = {AI-generated contextual summary: the treatise around which this whole dossier turns. Trithemius, a reforming Benedictine abbot, wrote In Praise of Scribes to persuade his monks NOT to abandon the manual copying of texts now that printing (from the 1450s) made books cheap and abundant. Three arguments recur and map onto the questionnaire: (1) DURABILITY (ch.~7) — 'the word written on parchment will last a thousand years; the printed book is a thing of paper and in a short time will decay entirely' — a quasi-empirical, precautionary case against the unproven new medium (Speed-precaution, Optimism-scepticism, a rationalised Control claim); (2) SPIRITUAL DISCIPLINE (ch.~3, ch.~6) — copying is the manual labour most fitting for monks, it fixes the text in the mind ('those things which we write we more firmly impress upon the mind') and illuminates the soul, so print threatens a formative practice, not just a craft (the moral/emotional pole of RAT, the Transhumanism reading of copying as mind-augmentation); (3) SERVICE TO CHURCH AND POSTERITY — scribes 'lend strength to words, memory to things, vigour to time', preserving faith for future generations (Altruism/benefit-sharing, Trust in the ecclesial-monastic institution). The performative tension is essential and is kept in the profile: Trithemius HAD the work printed, expanded the Sponheim library from ~48 to ~2000 volumes including printed books, and had his own works printed — so Openness and Optimism are documented off the floor. Used as the load-bearing anchor across all nine axes; verbatim wording graded via reproduction of the Behrendt/Salo translations, never fabricated.},
